In this lesson we'll look at how exponentials and logarithms are inverse operations. Think of it like addition and subtraction, or squaring and square roots — they undo each other. There are two key identities to remember. First, if you take a number, take a log of it in some base, then raise that base to that power, you end up right back where you started. Second, if you take a number, raise some base to that power, then take a log of the answer in the same base, you also land back where you started. The two functions are perfect mirrors of each other. Visually, that mirror shows up as a reflection across the diagonal line, so the graph of a log curve is just the exponential curve flipped. Both curves always pass through the point where the input is one and the output is zero, since any base raised to zero is one, and the log of one is zero. They also share an axis the exponential can never cross, and the log can never reach. The ranges and domains get swapped in the process, which is the classic fingerprint of inverse functions. Now let's see it in action.